1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,800
They carved a warning into a stone
and set it halfway up a mountain.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,500
Do not build your homes
below this point.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,200
You see, this was a tiny
fishing village in Japan,

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:16,800
and the sea had already
wiped it out. Twice.

5
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:19,800
In 1896, only two people survived.
In 1933, only four.

6
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:25,500
So the survivors put up the stone
and moved every house above it.

7
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,500
But up there, life is harder.

8
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:34,000
The walk down to the boats
is long and steep.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,200
Every single morning,
carrying nets.

10
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:39,800
And along the coast, other towns build
right on the water, behind big new sea walls.

11
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:43,000
Flat land, easy roads.
They call it progress.

12
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,500
To outsiders, the little village
looks stuck in the past.

13
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:49,800
Why live on a mountain
when the sea has a wall?

14
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:53,200
But nobody in the village
builds below the stone.

15
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:55,800
Not their children.
Not their grandchildren.

16
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,800
Then, March 11th, 2011.
The ground shakes for minutes.

17
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:04,000
Down the coast, the sea walls
were built for a big wave.

18
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,900
This one is bigger.

19
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:09,600
It pours over them
like they aren't there.

20
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,200
It rushes up the valley
toward the little village.

21
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:16,000
Past the fields. Past the road.
Still climbing.

22
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:19,800
And it stops about 300 feet
below the stone.

23
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:23,800
Every home in the village
was above that line.

24
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,500
Because the people who'd seen it twice
wrote down exactly where the water ends.
